<.shell current={:wallet_badges}>
  <h1>Wallet badges</h1>
  <p class="lead">Apple's and Google's own Add to Wallet badges.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <.wallet_badges id="wallet-badges-demo" apple_href="#" google_href="#" />
  </div>

  <h3>Once the card is in Wallet</h3>
  <div class="demo max-w-sm">
    <p class="mb-2"><strong>Your ID card is in Wallet.</strong></p>
    <details id="wallet-badges-demo-fold" class="disclosure">
      <summary class="hint">
        Add to another phone <.icon name="chevron-down" size={:sm} />
      </summary>
      <.wallet_badges id="wallet-badges-demo-again" class="mt-2" apple_href="#" google_href="#" />
    </details>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    Wherever a member adds their own ID card to a phone: the card's page from its email, Your membership, and the Dashboard's to-do. They are the only way, so a member sees the same two badges everywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>
    A page for team or site admins, such as Adminland's Integrations test. A pass there would land on the admin's phone, so it's a
    <a href={page_path(:button)}>button</a>
    that says what it tests.
  </p>

  <h2>Brand rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Both are the brands' own files, unchanged: Apple's English badge and Google's condensed Canadian English button. Never redraw, recolour, or crop them, and never put them in a <code>&lt;.button&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The same height, 48px, Google's minimum. Google's condensed button is two lines like Apple's, so the pair fits on one line on a 375px phone. Apple has no wider badge for the web.
    </li>
    <li>No other button on the page is bigger than them, as Google asks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Once the card is in Wallet</h2>
  <p>
    The badges draw the eye, so Your membership folds them away once SAR Duty knows the card is in Wallet: a phone registered the Apple pass, or Google said it was saved. Add to another phone opens them again, for a lost phone or a second one. The card's page always shows them, and the Dashboard's to-do leaves the list.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.wallet_badges id="…" apple_href=&#123;…&#125; google_href=&#123;…&#125; /&gt;</code>. Each href is a route that builds the pass when tapped. A badge shows only when its Wallet is set up. The fold is a <code>&lt;details class="disclosure"&gt;</code>, with no script.
  </p>
</.shell>
